---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const pumpCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'hr', label: 'heart rate (beats/min)', min: 40, max: 200, step: 1, value: 72, unit: 'bpm' },
    { name: 'sv', label: 'stroke volume (mL/beat)', min: 40, max: 150, step: 1, value: 70, unit: 'mL' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'cardiac output', expr: 'hr*sv/1000', unit: 'L/min' },
    { label: 'pumped per day', expr: 'hr*sv*1440/1000', unit: 'L' },
    { label: 'beats per day', expr: 'hr*1440' },
  ],
};

const circuitCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'body → vena cava → right atrium', note: 'Oxygen-poor blood returns from the tissues and drains into the heart\'s upper-right chamber.' },
    { math: 'right atrium → right ventricle → lungs', note: 'Through the tricuspid valve, out through the pulmonary artery — the only artery that carries oxygen-poor blood.' },
    { math: 'lungs: CO₂ out, O₂ in', note: 'Gas exchange across capillary walls one cell thick. The blood brightens from dark red to scarlet.' },
    { math: 'lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta', note: 'Through the mitral valve into the heart\'s strongest chamber — its wall is ~3× thicker — and out to the whole body. One full circuit; "lub-dub" is the valves slamming shut.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/foundational/heart-circulation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every cell in your body lives bathed in fluid and needs a constant delivery service —
    oxygen in, carbon dioxide out, glucose and hormones dropped off, waste collected. Only the
    outermost layer of cells can absorb directly from the air or swap across a surface, so
    somewhere inside you there must be a courier network. That network is the cardiovascular
    system: a pump, two loops of plumbing, and 5 litres of cargo. The heart is a double pump —
    the right half feeds the <strong>lungs</strong>, the left half feeds the <strong>body</strong>
    — and the two halves beat in lockstep about 100,000 times a day. Set it to a resting
    72 beats per minute and it moves roughly 5 litres of blood every minute: about 7,300 litres
    a day, through ~100,000 km of vessels. William Harvey proved this loop in 1628
    (<em>De Motu Cordis</em>) by a disarmingly simple argument — the heart's capacity times its
    rate pushes more blood in an hour than the whole body contains, so the blood must
    <em>circulate</em>, not ebb like a tide — and founded modern physiology on the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson follows one red blood cell on its full circuit: out of the heart's left side,
    through arteries, into capillary beds where it surrenders its oxygen, back through veins,
    through the right side, out to the lungs for a new load, and home again — a round trip of
    about a minute, repeated some three billion times in a lifetime. Along the way we meet the
    cargo (blood), the pump (the heart), the pipes (vessels), the drainage system (lymphatics)
    and the loading dock (lungs).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pump's arithmetic — drag heart rate and stroke volume like exercise or shock would" widget="sliderLab" config={pumpCfg} />

  <p>
    Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. Rest: 72 × 70 mL ≈ 5 L/min. In hard exercise
    both knobs rise — trained athletes reach 20–30 L/min, a five-fold increase. In shock,
    output falls despite the racing pulse: fast, empty beats. The numbers, not the drumbeat,
    tell the story.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood: the cargo</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is a tissue — about 45% cells suspended in 55% <strong>plasma</strong>, a pale yellow
    water-and-protein solution carrying glucose, amino acids, hormones, clotting factors,
    antibodies and the dissolved wastes headed for the kidneys. The cell fraction splits into
    three jobs. <strong>Erythrocytes</strong> (red cells) are concave discs with no nucleus,
    packing ~270 million molecules of haemoglobin each — the iron-containing protein that
    binds oxygen in the lungs and releases it where concentrations fall. Their cargo capacity
    is the reason a person can survive with lungs that only extract a fifth of the air's
    oxygen. <strong>Leucocytes</strong> (white cells) are the immune patrol: neutrophils as
    first-response bacteria-killers, lymphocytes as the targeted intelligence service,
    monocytes and the rest as cleanup and coordination. <strong>Platelets</strong> are cell
    fragments that trigger clotting — a cascade that turns a leaking vessel wall into a
    fibrin plug within minutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Red cells are consumables: each lives ~120 days, and your marrow builds about two million
    replacements per second. Haemoglobin's iron is the reason anaemia (too little working
    haemoglobin) shows up as fatigue and pallor — the delivery fleet is running under capacity.
  </p>

  <h2>The heart: the pump</h2>
  <p>
    The heart is a fist-sized muscle with four chambers arranged as two pumps in series.
    <strong>Atria</strong> are the receiving chambers; <strong>ventricles</strong> the power
    chambers; four one-way valves — tricuspid and mitral between atrium and ventricle, pulmonic
    and aortic at the exits — keep flow moving forward only. The "lub-dub" you hear through a
    stethoscope is literally the valves slamming shut: first the atrioventricular pair at the
    start of ventricular contraction (<strong>systole</strong>), then the outlet valves as the
    ventricles refill (<strong>diastole</strong>). The left ventricle does far more work than
    the right — it must push blood to the toes, not just across the corridor to the lungs —
    and its wall is ~3× thicker to match.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most remarkably, the heart has its own electrical plant: a cluster of self-exciting cells
    in the right atrium, the <strong>sinoatrial (SA) node</strong>, fires roughly every 0.8 s
    and spreads a wave through the muscle; the <strong>atrioventricular (AV) node</strong> then
    relays it down the ventricle walls with a built-in delay so the atria finish emptying
    first. This is why a heart beats even outside the body, and why a pacemaker can take over
    with a single timed wire. The heart muscle has its own private supply — the
    <strong>coronary arteries</strong>, fed at the very exit of the aorta so they fill during
    diastole. Narrow them (atherosclerosis) and the muscle starves during effort: that is a
    heart attack. The single biggest preventable risks remain smoking, hypertension, lipids
    and inactivity.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood vessels and the double circulation</h2>
  <p>
    The plumbing is a one-way gradient of pressure. <strong>Arteries</strong> carry blood away
    from the heart under high pressure; thick elastic walls stretch with each pulse and recoil
    between beats, smoothing the flow (the exception everyone learns: the pulmonary artery
    carries oxygen-poor blood, the pulmonary vein oxygen-rich — the naming is by direction, not
    content). <strong>Arterioles</strong> are the adjustable taps: rings of smooth muscle
    widen or narrow each supply line, which is how blood is re-routed — to skin for cooling,
    to gut after a meal, to muscle in a sprint. <strong>Capillaries</strong> are where
    business happens: tubes one endothelial cell thick, so narrow that red cells pass in
    single file, slowed to maximise exchange. Then pressure falls away, and
    <strong>veins</strong> — thin-walled, low-pressure, with one-way valves and the squeeze of
    your leg muscles — carry blood back uphill to the right atrium.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Blood pressure</strong> is written systole-over-diastole — 120/80 mmHg is the
    classic resting reference: artery pressure at the squeeze vs the refill. Total blood
    volume is ≈ 5 litres; a full circuit of the body at rest takes about a minute, while the
    lung loop takes about ten seconds. That asymmetry is the point of <strong>double
    circulation</strong>: the body loop is long and high-pressure, the lung loop short and
    low-pressure, so each pump is sized for its job rather than one pump straining through
    both.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="one full lap of a blood cell — the double circulation in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={circuitCfg} />

  <h2>The lymphatic and immune system: the drainage and the defence</h2>
  <p>
    Capillaries leak. Every day, roughly 20 litres of plasma water is forced out into the
    tissues, and only about 17 litres is reabsorbed directly — the missing 3 litres would
    become fatal swelling within a day if nothing collected it. The <strong>lymphatic
    system</strong> is the drainage: blind-ended lymph capillaries that soak up the excess
    fluid (now called <strong>lymph</strong>), carry it through lymphatic vessels with their
    own one-way valves, and return it to the bloodstream near the collarbones. Along the way,
    lymph percolates through <strong>lymph nodes</strong> — bean-shaped filters packed with
    lymphocytes. That positioning is no accident: the drainage system doubles as customs
    control. When you feel "swollen glands" in your neck during a throat infection, the nodes
    are enlarging because their immune cells are multiplying to meet the invader they have
    trapped.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gives the lymphatics their second role: immunity. <strong>Innate</strong> defences
    (skin, fever, inflammation, neutrophils) respond fast and non-specifically;
    <strong>adaptive</strong> immunity — B lymphocytes making antibodies, T lymphocytes
    killing infected cells and coordinating — learns each pathogen and remembers it, which is
    the principle vaccination exploits: train the adaptive system on a harmless preview so the
    real pathogen meets a standing army. The spleen filters blood the way nodes filter lymph,
    recycling worn red cells.
  </p>

  <h2>The respiratory system: the loading dock</h2>
  <p>
    Circulation is only half the story if the cargo never gets loaded. Air enters nose and
    mouth, is warmed, humidified and filtered, and branches through trachea, bronchi and
    bronchioles into ~300 million <strong>alveoli</strong> — grape-like air sacs giving a
    combined exchange surface of roughly 70 m², wrapped in the same capillary net the right
    heart has just delivered blood to. Oxygen diffuses across two cell-thicknesses into the
    blood and onto haemoglobin; carbon dioxide diffuses the other way and is exhaled.
    <strong>Ventilation</strong> — breathing — is bulk flow driven by pressure: the diaphragm
    contracts and flattens, chest volume grows, pressure falls below atmospheric, and air
    floods in (inhaling is mostly suction, not swallowing).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> blood is not blue in the veins. Oxygen-poor blood is dark
    red; veins look bluish through skin because of how light penetrates tissue. And "used"
    blood is not empty — it still carries about 75% of the oxygen it had in the lungs at rest,
    a deliberate reserve the body draws on when demand spikes.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative elegance is worth seeing. Oxygen delivery is the product of the knobs you
    met above: cardiac output × haemoglobin concentration × saturation. At rest that is
    ~5 L/min × 150 g Hb/L × ~1.34 mL O₂/g ≈ 1,000 mL of oxygen per minute, close to resting
    consumption (~250 mL/min) — the system runs with roughly fourfold headroom, which is the
    reserve called on in exercise. Haemoglobin's affinity is the deeper trick: it binds oxygen
    tightly at lung oxygen levels and releases it in tissues, shifted further right by heat
    and acidity — so exercising muscle, warm and CO₂-rich, literally induces its own delivery
    (the Bohr effect). Fetal haemoglobin is tuned slightly higher still, to poach oxygen
    across the placenta.
  </p>
  <p>
    Regulation is a control loop, not a metronome. Medullary chemoreceptors watch CO₂ (via
    cerebrospinal-fluid pH) and stretch receptors watch the lungs; heart rate and breathing
    adjust within seconds. Baroreceptors in the carotid arteries sense blood pressure and
    adjust heart rate and vessel tone continuously — the reason standing up quickly is
    compensated before you notice. When regulation fails, the failure modes are the major
    killers: atherosclerosis narrowing coronaries (ischaemia, infarction), valves leaking or
    stiffening (murmurs as turbulence), conduction system short-circuiting (arrhythmias), and
    the lymphatic-vascular line congesting (oedema, heart failure). Nearly all of these are
    visible in the physics you have already met — pressures, resistances and flows — which is
    why physiology is taught with equations and why "lub-dub" is worth listening to closely.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final layer ties the vessels to the tissues they serve. Capillary exchange is
    governed by a two-force tug (Starling's forces): blood pressure pushes fluid
    <em>out</em> of the arterial end of each capillary, while plasma proteins (chiefly
    albumin) pull water back <em>in</em> by osmosis at the venous end — filter at one
    end, reclaim at the other, with the lymphatics collecting the difference. The
    clinical failures map onto the forces one-to-one: lose plasma protein (malnutrition,
    liver disease) and fluid floods the tissues (oedema); raise the outward pressure
    (hypertension, venous blockage) and the same swelling follows from the other side.
    It is the whole transport system in one equation — pressure versus osmosis across a
    wall one cell thick.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Harvey, De Motu Cordis (1628); Guyton &amp; Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Tortora &amp; Derrickson, Principles of Anatomy and Physiology; British Heart Foundation patient resources.</p>
</TopicPage>
